    "1:_":
      "Before he begins, etc. And before he checks he should purify his hands even without rubbing, and this is not grossness of spirit. Maharil; nevertheless through covering his feet and rubbing is better (Maharshal in responsum siman 86). And he blesses Who has sanctified us, etc., on biur chametz. The reason why in some blessings they formulated in the singular and in some in plural with a lamed as in al avodat Hashem — and it is implied in the Gemara and in Ashrei first chapter of Pesachim that if he blessed to destroy chametz he fulfilled, and so in Ta'anit Netiv 5 part 1. And that one does not bless first to destroy chametz is because one need not leave crumbs of chametz, for a person's intent is on biur if found; therefore they say on biur and not to destroy, which implies he will certainly destroy chametz — perhaps he will not find chametz as stated (and further that from here one does not bless to destroy) because it implies certainly for the future — namely because from his blessings it is recognizable if he is a Torah scholar; through this it is recognizable that he is a Torah scholar and knows that on biur also the future is implied, as they say in Berachot chapter 2: Rava praised R' Zeira that he is a great man expert in blessings, etc.; he opened and said Who brings forth bread from the earth; they said this one of whom they said he is a great man, etc. — what does he teach us? Evidently one must make known to us a novelty in his blessings. Maharshal wonders why he does not bless then Shehecheyanu on the night; Bach reconciles — and see what I wrote above siman 29 that it appears to me to distinguish regarding Shehecheyanu specifically when it comes for joy of a person's heart that he merited this time to fulfill the mitzva — in that case certainly he may bless, as we say in the chapter Kol me'arvin a new reading; but regarding other blessings one does not bless where there is dispute of the great ones. Nevertheless our rabbi answered our rabbi the Gaon above siman 69 regarding the blessing to guard His commandments — there it is certainly a blessing in vain; and for the law it is fit and proper not to enter oneself into doubt of blessing; and so the practice is not to bless Shehecheyanu on bedikat chametz.",
